as32v -- the 32V VAX assembler on a modern host
================================================

32V's `as' (usr/src/cmd/as: as0.y, as1.c, as2.c, as3.c, as.h, instrs) built as a host program, so
VAX assembly (from the VAX pcc, or any other source) can be assembled and measured without booting
32V.  It reads the same syntax and writes the same a.out (magic 0410) as the original.

Installed beside the V7 assembler: `libexec/v7unix/as32v`, and `as32v` in the bin directory.

Changes from the 32V sources (as0.y, as3.c and instrs are unchanged):

  * the program keeps pointers in ints (yacc values, symbol-table links), so it is a 32-bit build
    (-m32); `yyfix.sed` gives each `->` on a yacc value the struct it always meant (V7 yacc's
    values are ints, which old C allowed to be dereferenced);
  * as.h: `instab[]` is `extern` (the table is defined in as3.c);
  * as1.c: the symbol table is one calloc'd block (the host's sbrk and malloc must not mix);
    `mktemp` on a string literal is replaced by pid-based names; the stdio buffers shared through
    _sibuf/_sobuf (a PDP-11 memory saving) are dropped;
  * as2.c: members of the overlaid struct tshdr are accessed through it, not through symtab;
  * a.out.h is 32V's, because the host has no VAX a.out.

Use: as32v [-o out.o] [-d] [-L] [-V] [-W] file.s ...   (as in 32V's as(1))

The yacc input is run through the V7 yacc of this tree (`modern/usr/src/cmd/yacc`).
`make check` assembles tests/addl.s and checks the header and the code bytes.
